## 3.2.2  Sn‑based MOF
Sn-based MOFs are another class of catalysts with excellent HCOOH selectivity. Geng et al. [123] prepared Sn-doped ZIF8 catalysts via an ion-exchange strategy, the method can efficiently integrate Sn into the node of ZIF-8 while preserving the whole framework structure. When tested in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated $0.5\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ , Sn-doped ZIF8 showed high HCOOH activity and selectivity of $74\%$ FE at $-1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE. And the spatially separated Sn atoms are proposed to be responsible for the superior ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ activity of $\mathrm{Sn}$ -doped ZIF8. Similarly, Deng et al. [124] also replaces the Zn metal nodes in ZIF8 by Sn doping, and then follows a solventassisted linker exchange (SALE) process to obtain the SnN6-MOF catalyst (Fig. 10a). When tested in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated 0.5 M ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ , such catalyst achieved excellent selectivity for HCOOH with $\mathrm{FE_{HCOOH}\ u p}$ to $85.1\%$ at $-1.23\mathrm{~V~}\nu_{S}$ . RHE. In-situ Raman spectra indicate the organic ligands in MOF are gradually lost during the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ (Fig. 10b). Furthermore, ex-situ $^{119}\mathrm{Sn}$ Mössbauer results demonstrated the presence of zero-valence Sn metal after continuous electrolysis for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ at $-1.23{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE (Fig. 10c). A series of experiments results have revealed that Sn-N6-MOF catalyst undergoes in-situ structural reconstruction during the ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ process and then generates Sn nanoclusters, which are the real active sites for producing HCOOH (Fig. 10d). In a previous study, $\mathrm{w}_{\mathrm{u}}$ et al. [125] also discovered that $\mathrm{Sn}(101)$ crystal plane is more favorable for the formation of HCOOH (Fig. 10e). They synthesized a Sn-MOF $(\mathrm{Sn}_{3}\mathrm{O}(1,4{\-}\mathrm{BDC})_{2})$ precursor to prepare a series of $S_{\mathrm{n(101)}}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composites via the calcination and acidic etching processes (Fig. 10f). The $\mathrm{Sn(101)/SnO_{2}/C{-}}500$ (500 is the carbonization temperature) exhibits high selectivity toward HCOOH and achieved a $\mathrm{FE_{HCOOH}}$ up to $93.3\%$ at $-0.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs. RHE (Fig. $10\mathrm{g}\backslash$ ). However, the $\mathrm{FE_{HCOOH}}$ of such catalyst is gradually reduced from $93.3\%$ to $72.8\%$ after continuous electrolysis for $13\mathrm{h}$ at $-0.8\mathrm{~V~}\nu_{S}$ . RHE.
## 3.2.3  Bi‑based MOF
Bi is also a promising electrocatalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ to produce formate in aqueous solutions because of its large overpotential for HER in the aqueous electrolyte (Bi is situated at the bottom corner point of the volcano plot), low toxicity, and good stability [138, 139]. Therefore, Bi-based MOFs have drawn tremendous attention as catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to HCOOH [140]. Zhang et al. [126] synthesized a Bi-MOF (Bi-BTC-D) by the hydrothermal method and evaluated its ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}$ performance in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated $0.5\mathrm{~M~KHCO}_{3}$ electrolyte. Electrochemical experiments showed HCOOH selectivity with a maximum FE of $95.5\%$ at $-0.86\mathrm{V}$ vs. RHE. DFT calculations revealed that BTC ligands in MOF structure can effectively regulate the catalytic activity of the Bi atoms. Li et al. [127] also use the same ligand $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC)}$ to construct a helical rod-based 2D Bi-MOF (CAU-17) with permanent crystallographic-independent channels (Fig. 11a), and it exhibits high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -to-HCOOH activity in the H-cell. Such catalyst achieves an excellent FE of $92.2\%$ at $-0.9\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. RHE (Fig.  11b, c). It is worth noting that the type of catholyte significantly affects the selectivity of HCOOH (Fig. 11d). When ${\mathrm{SO}}_{4}^{2-}$ was added in the catholyte, the accumulated negative charges on the electrode surface can generate a potential difference in the electric double layer and facilitates the transport of polar water molecules instead of nonpolar $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, leading to the dominating competitive HER. Operando XAS (Fig. 11e) and DFT calculations were also conducted to explore the origin of the high HCOOH selectivity on the Bi-MOF, and the results showed that the highly accessible $\mathbf{Bi}^{3+}$ and the unique channels played vital roles in the enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and HCOOH conversion. Nevertheless, the maximum H